Comparative Literature Review Paragraph  

Recent advances in supply chain optimization have explored hybrid machine learning architectures to mitigate the bullwhip effect. For instance, Optimizing Multi-Tier Supply Chain Ordering with a Hybrid Liquid Neural Network and Extreme Gradient Boosting Model (arXiv:2512.14112) demonstrated that combining continuous-time adaptability (via Liquid Neural Networks) with global feature optimization (via XGBoost) yields superior profitability and reduced variance amplification compared to LSTM, Transformer, and reinforcement learning baselines. Their simulation-based findings highlight the importance of volatility monitoring and inventory–sales heuristics as stabilizing signals. In contrast, the Agentic Bullwhip V2 experiment shifts the focus from hybrid model architectures to agentic coordination strategies, emphasizing decentralized decision-making and autonomous agent interactions. This positions Agentic Bullwhip V2 as a complementary extension of the literature—moving from algorithmic optimization toward agent-based governance of supply chain dynamics.

