BULL‑ODE: Bullwhip Learning with Neural ODEs and Universal Differential Equations under Stochastic Demand  
Srinivasan et al. (2025) introduce a novel framework that applies Neural Ordinary Differential Equations (NODEs) and Universal Differential Equations (UDEs) to model bullwhip dynamics under stochastic demand. Their experiments reveal that UDEs, which embed structural constraints such as inventory conservation and order‑up‑to rules, outperform NODEs under autocorrelated and Gaussian demand regimes by delivering higher stability and accuracy. Conversely, NODEs excel under heavy‑tailed lognormal shocks, capturing rare extreme events more effectively. The study highlights a critical trade‑off: structure‑preserving models are advantageous when noise is modest and correlated, while flexible models are better suited to environments dominated by rare, extreme fluctuations. This contribution provides methodological guidance for supply chain researchers on when to enforce structural bias versus allow flexibility, complementing agent‑based approaches like Agentic Bullwhip V2 that emphasize decentralized coordination rather than purely mathematical structure.

