The Collaboration Paradox: Why Generative AI Requires Both Strategic Intelligence and Operational Stability in Supply Chain Management  
Dhar (2025) investigates the emergent dynamics of generative AI agents in a multi‑echelon supply chain simulation, focusing on whether collaborative AI mitigates or exacerbates the bullwhip effect. The study reveals a striking “collaboration paradox”: theoretically superior collaborative agents, designed with Vendor‑Managed Inventory (VMI) principles, performed worse than non‑AI baselines due to a hoarding effect that starved downstream nodes. This failure mode underscores that strategic intelligence alone is insufficient; resilience requires a synthesis of two layers—high‑level AI‑driven proactive policy‑setting and low‑level collaborative execution with proactive downstream replenishment. The final framework successfully generated, evaluated, and quantified portfolios of strategic choices, achieving near‑perfect service levels across disruption scenarios. The paper contributes both a cautionary narrative on the risks of naïve AI deployment and a blueprint for integrating generative AI into supply chain management, highlighting the need for operational stability alongside strategic intelligence.

