LMs for Supply Chain Management  
This paper (2025) introduces the first domain‑specialized large language model for supply chain management, enhanced with retrieval‑augmented generation (RAG) to integrate external knowledge. The authors demonstrate that the model achieves expert‑level competence by passing standardized SCM exams (SCMP, CPIM) and performing competitively in the beer game. Beyond exams, the study explores horizontal and vertical supply chain games: in Cournot and Bertrand competition, LLM agents converge toward equilibrium outcomes, while in multi‑tier vertical settings they reproduce classical bullwhip dynamics. Importantly, the analysis shows that risk preferences strongly influence bullwhip intensity—risk‑averse agents amplify demand variability most, while risk‑neutral agents minimize it. Information sharing across tiers reduces bullwhip variance dramatically, with up to 99% reduction at the manufacturer stage for risk‑averse agents. The contribution is twofold: it validates that RAG‑augmented LLMs can act as expert supply chain agents, and it provides new insights into how agentic preferences and information flows shape bullwhip dynamics. In relation to Agentic Bullwhip V2, this work establishes a methodological precedent for using LLMs as autonomous supply chain actors, while your experiment extends the literature by focusing on decentralized agent coordination strategies.

